The goal-translation pipeline follows lean-smt's design closely, and these files are
direct ports, each carrying a header saying what was changed:

    Vampire/Attribute.lean               <- Smt/Attribute.lean
    Vampire/Data/Graph.lean              <- Smt/Data/Graph.lean
    Vampire/Recognizers.lean             <- Smt/Recognizers.lean
    Vampire/Translate.lean               <- Smt/Translate.lean
    Vampire/Translate/Term.lean          <- Smt/Translate/Term.lean
    Vampire/Translate/Commands.lean      <- Smt/Translate/Commands.lean
    Vampire/Translate/Query.lean         <- Smt/Translate/Query.lean
    Vampire/Translate/Prop.lean          <- Smt/Translate/Prop.lean
    Vampire/Translate/Bool.lean          <- Smt/Translate/Bool.lean
    Vampire/Preprocess/Basic.lean        <- Smt/Preprocess/Basic.lean
    Vampire/Preprocess/Intros.lean       <- Smt/Preprocess/Intros.lean
    Vampire/Preprocess/NegateGoal.lean   <- Smt/Preprocess/NegateGoal.lean
    Vampire/Preprocess/PushHintsToCtx.lean <- Smt/Preprocess/PushHintsToCtx.lean
    Vampire/Preprocess/Mono.lean         <- Smt/Preprocess/Mono.lean

    Vampire/Tactic.lean                  follows Smt/Tactic/Smt.lean

Vampire/Translate/Build.lean has no counterpart: lean-smt renders its terms as SMT-LIB
text for a solver process, whereas this compiles them into an embedded Vampire's own
data structures.

Vampire/Preprocess/Mono.lean carries lean-smt's own variants of auto's lemma collection
along with the port, because monomorphisation is driven by calling into lean-auto and
those variants are what make the call. lean-smt declares them in lean-auto's `Auto`
namespace; here they are in `Vampire.Preprocess.Mono` instead, under the same
identifiers, so that a file can import this tactic and `smt` at once — two ports of one
file cannot both add the same names to a third library's namespace. lean-auto is used
under the Apache License 2.0:

